     2	"""Sandboxed Antigravity (``agy``) critic dispatch and its terminal STOP contract.
     3	
     4	Every agy launch, preflight included, runs inside a bubblewrap sandbox. The whole filesystem is bound
     5	read-only; the only writable surface is a fresh per-dispatch workspace outside every repository. The
     6	prompt and inputs are bound read-only into that workspace. Host service sockets are masked (herdr,
     7	``/run/user/<uid>``, ``/tmp``) and the environment is an allowlist. agy's own ``--sandbox`` flag is not an
     8	isolation boundary (2026-07-19/20 incidents), so this module never falls back to an unsandboxed launch.
     9	
    10	Attribution is structural, not timing-based:
    11	
    12	* a write retained in the critic workspace is a critic mutation -> ``AgyStop(kind="critic")``;
    13	* evidence that the boundary itself failed (the in-sandbox write probe succeeds, or the read-only
    14	  sentinel changes) -> ``AgyStop(kind="boundary_failure")``;
    15	* a change in the watched repository during the window belongs to a co-tenant: it is logged to
    16	  ``windows.jsonl`` and never reverted, blamed on the critic, or halting;
    17	* write attempts the kernel blocked (visible in agy stderr) are logged, not halting.
    18	
    19	A STOP is terminal. ``AgyStop`` derives from ``BaseException`` so no ``except Exception`` handler can
    20	absorb or retry it. It latches the process, and a durable record in the repository's git common dir
    21	blocks every worktree of that repository in any later process until a logged operator clear. Incident
    22	directories are the authority: a missing ``stop.json`` with an uncleared incident is a ``tamper`` STOP.
    23	
    24	Refusals (``AgyDispatchRefusedError``: sandbox unavailable, bwrap setup failure, no git repository) launch
    25	no critic and write no durable record. Operator rulings: orchestration/redirect-focused-fix-2026-09-24.md.
    26	"""
